# FABLE-SONNET-OCC-ROOT-20260924-146 commit 2 / D-25: orphan, measured and
# completed -- the original never called go(), so this was untested either
# way. @: n (address of a plain own-declared local, not a formal) into a
# pointer parameter, write-back through it -- read two ways to prove the
# write itself landed, not just that go() returns some number.
#
# set_one reads \p right after writing it (through the SAME taken address)
# and reports that: proves the write reaches real storage. go's own
# `return: n` afterward is the ordinary BARE working-copy read, and per L2
# spec 18.2's own worked example ("p: @x, \p: 9 leaves bare working x at
# 5") that stays 0 -- a write through a taken address reaches the GRAPH
# cell directly; the working-copy cache is not refreshed by it, unlike a
# PARAMETER's own-binding rule (18.3), where the working cell IS the
# canonical one. `n` is a plain local, not a parameter -- contrast the
# sibling unit_addr_arg.lm2, identical shape but with n as a formal, which
# reads 1U back through the bare parameter precisely because that
# canonical-cell rule applies there and does not apply here.
fn: set_one (@: size_t p) int
    \p: 1U
    if: \p != 1U
        return: 82
    return: 0
end: set_one
fn: go () int
    size_t: n
    n: 0U
    if: set_one(@: n) != 0
        return: 82
    return: n
end: go

int: got
got: go()
if: got != 0
    sendMessage: exit(exit_code: 81; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
    return
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: 0; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
